Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Slab chunks and cache objects as Sublet lifetimes (CDERIVE/CREVOKE/CRENEW)
Upstream: memcached 1.6.45
Upstream-Archive: https://memcached.org/files/memcached-1.6.45.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: d362c64e6d8d5287153501eabf7c85b4a761432fbf53f5d7b085d0bb1653c1dd
Build-Variants: only with -DMC_CAPSTONE_SUBLET, on the virtual profile (musl mallocng, object
  lifetimes on), with -I capstone/runtime/include (capability.h); every other build is unchanged
Depends-On: memcached-1.6.45-0001-capability-alignment.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

memcached's slab allocator never frees a chunk: do_slabs_free files it on its class's free list
and do_slabs_alloc hands it out again, and cache.c keeps freed objects on a list the same way. A
stale item or buffer pointer therefore reaches whatever the chunk holds next. Three instructions,
inserted where the allocators hand memory out and take it back, end that
(docs/design/virtual-capstone/isa.md): CDERIVE makes a child capability of a non-linear parent,
bounded to a sub-range; CREVOKE(object) ends the object its one operand names and everything
derived inside it, and faults when that object is not live; CRENEW(object) ends it the same way
and begins a new lifetime in the same place. Neither names a parent or needs a right.

Slabs. The slab lists hold each page as memory_allocate returns it. Each page carries one
lifetime, the generation:
- split_slab_page_into_freelist derives the generation for the class the page now serves, and
  writes it into every chunk header (item.sublet_parent; item_chunk has the same slot, so the two
  layouts stay in step and every item grows by one capability).
- do_slabs_alloc returns a child of that generation bounded to the class's chunk size.
- slabs_free reads the generation through the caller's own pointer, ends the caller's chunk and
  passes do_slabs_free a pointer at the same address derived from the generation. The chained
  chunks of a large item end the same way in do_slabs_free_chunked.
- slabs_finalize_page_move ends the page's generation when the page leaves its class. That
  ends, in one instruction, every chunk the page handed out, including the ones the mover rescued
  or reclaimed in place without a free.

Chunks are bounded to the chunk size and not to the request: slabs_alloc is not told the request,
and the mover copies whole chunks (memcpy of cls_size into a chunk it allocated). A page's own
pointer has the bounds memory_allocate returns: the page when each page is one malloc, the whole
block when pages are cut from a preallocated one (-L, which no recipe of this port passes).

The mover walks a page through the page's own pointer. That pointer names the malloc'd memory and
not a chunk, so a CREVOKE through it would end every chunk in that memory at once. For a linked
item (MOVE_FROM_LRU, MOVE_BUSY_ACTIVE) the mover therefore takes the hash table's reference to it
under the item lock, and every unlink, replace or remove that may free it uses that reference. Its
three whole-chunk copies keep the destination's own parent.

Cache. Every cache object is one malloc, so it already has a lifetime: the one the system
allocator gave it. cache_alloc hands out the pointer malloc returned, as upstream does.
cache_free renews that lifetime (CRENEW), which ends every copy of the caller's pointer, and
files the renewed pointer on the free list. cache_destroy passes free() that pointer, which
names the allocation's current lifetime. The layout of an object is upstream's, with or without
the debug redzone. This relies on the system allocator giving each object a lifetime of its
own: on a heap without object lifetimes the pointer would name something larger, and renewing
it would end that.

What it gives up: a live item pointer can read the item header, as it could without the patch,
and so can load the generation, which has page-wide bounds and whose CREVOKE ends every chunk of
the page. A stale pointer cannot, since it is revoked. Overwriting that slot clears its tag and
the next free faults. Restart mode (-e) rebuilds item pointers from addresses, so the items it
restores are not children of a generation and their first free faults.

